WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.155
The Sieve of Eratosthenes marks composite numbers up to N in O(N log log N) time.

00:00:06.155 --> 00:00:16.185
Start crossing out multiples of each prime p at p squared because any smaller composite multiple already has a prime factor below p.

00:00:16.185 --> 00:00:22.720
In Python, assign a zero bytearray slice in one step to run the inner loop at C speed.

00:00:23.033 --> 00:00:31.282
Precomputing the Smallest Prime Factor for every integer up to N unlocks ultra-fast repeated factorizations.

00:00:31.282 --> 00:00:36.934
To factor any number x, repeatedly divide x by spf[x] until x reaches one.

00:00:36.934 --> 00:00:44.953
Thousands of factorization queries finish in logarithmic time each instead of square-root trial division.

00:00:45.267 --> 00:00:51.330
When a single number reaches ten to the twelfth, a full array sieve cannot fit in memory.

00:00:51.330 --> 00:01:00.186
Divide out factors d up to the square root of n; if the remaining value exceeds one after the loop, that cofactor is prime itself.

00:01:00.186 --> 00:01:07.067
For sixty-four-bit primality checks, deterministic Miller-Rabin bases test primality in microseconds.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:15.168
A segmented sieve finds all primes in a narrow interval from L to R even when R reaches ten to the twelfth.

00:01:15.168 --> 00:01:23.226
First sieve base primes up to the square root of R, then offset each base prime into the shifted window buffer.

00:01:23.226 --> 00:01:27.291
Only the window of size R minus L plus one is allocated.
